329a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072736 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{2}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072736 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072736 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "babfb277-5468-5104-adcd-6c9138b79ae1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072737 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{3}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072737 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072737 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f36c0d2c-fa6f-5dcd-b33a-dda9aa89064d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072738 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{4} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{1}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072738 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072738 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1bb2bca0-9b88-5195-9025-2c090a814e87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072739 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{3}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072739 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072739 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d7aa5a7-623c-5259-9cd3-81c3e1b4ee2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073417 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073417 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8f/8fd479debb08b47c49552e543d2a8a53d0cdcbfb59c9a43333b7650f33bd063a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Write down this multiplication as a repeated addition of fraction models." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073417 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3241956-5b5b-5760-9383-d7da13f48b16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073417 #edb/ref 1074 "In the model, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{3}\n$$\n\nmultiplied by ${\\color{blue}3}$. This means that we need to make $\\color{blue}3$ copies of the given fraction model.\n\n![](images/38/38d0f24ab003913a30051d64dda233980173939825d9cc6f34473b1cbdcdbeed.png)\n\nSo, the missing fraction model is:\n\n![](images/ac/ac8618a503eb2cfd41a7fa8851cdb0dbb7152325100ec922b533d9da6e63b7c5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073418 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10549" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073418 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5a/5aef67e5f151465f09eba64456f4566cc984c97857d8e6203cd25d60952e183a.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{5}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073418 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eea25500-f78e-544f-8bf9-7eaacfac84a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073418 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{5}\n$$\n\nby $\\color{blue}3$, we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}3$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe41721739d82df825d2a7e28bc19590fbc784354a58aa74137aceda0e6be202.png)\n\nThere are\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3} \\times {\\color{red}1} = 3\n$$\n\nshaded pieces in total. Therefore,\n\n$$\n3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{5} = \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073419 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10550" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073419 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e7/e71724d5a2f16bd88fdae52e2717ec65176ff82bc74e92cd7e18a2a19f1b8218.png)\n\nWhat multiplication problem is represented by the model above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073419 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c074473-dc1c-56c6-825b-be4b6b3dbfa2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073419 #edb/ref 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$\n\non the left and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$\n\non the right.\n\n![](images/ee/eef3cec4311b03e7f4be59e367cbd3d6947afd73b16a00b487f28ae7ea2bf8b8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9} \\times 4 = \\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We can swap the factors on the left-hand side. So, the following would be a correct answer too:\n\n$$\n4 \\times \\dfrac{1}{9} = \\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073420 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10555" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073420 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e9/e9bc9f6b014b092a363dbac6ff64bedefe26846c34851fd5029c89af61d68d8f.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the multiplication problem above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073420 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e10607a-5812-50cd-a2d5-4b7c3ebcbde1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073420 #edb/ref 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\non the left and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\non the right.\n\n![](images/c1/c1efa667a938907fcdd5ba9d0172b37a7efb0a7b312e3025556c4444814d2ab3.png)\n\nThe shape on the left has $1$ shaded piece, and the shape on the right has $3$ shaded pieces. So the number of shaded pieces has increased by a factor of ${\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5} \\times {\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}3}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073795 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073795 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073795 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073795 #edb/ref 1039 "Representing a Product as a Repeated Addition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073795 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073795 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "95e51046-fc4c-536b-91eb-82c7c298b7be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073796 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073796 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073796 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073796 #edb/ref 1039 "Multiplying a Unit Fraction by a Whole Number Using Repeated Addition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073796 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073796 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c1e7017e-45df-5adf-9ded-3f815b4f5673" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073797 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying a Multiplication Problem Given a Model" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073797 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073797 