9 "q-60128" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074408 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/74/7476c8699798c46fa643ec6c4d87d01056aef9581fa02a02cbc38ac46a06005a.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the multiplication problem above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074408 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b4796c8-acd0-5d6d-aed3-4a2cf12b76d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074416 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186072734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074416 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074416 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/77/774cdfd02bee2620b12a7e34c3ef928e721a82d87433c8265ea3e433731b52fc.png)\n\nWhat multiplication problem is represented by the model above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074416 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3b5381ee-a86a-57b9-b149-a19f163d14c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074736 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074736 #edb/ref 1061 39867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074736 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cf5a7b59-9df0-505a-86e0-c7437dc8b262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074736 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074737 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074737 #edb/ref 1061 39868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074737 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f29f88a5-25e3-54f3-b724-70c3d82de050" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074737 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074738 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074738 #edb/ref 1061 39869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074738 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec8f3fd6-e68a-5a08-909a-7ca8734e46ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074738 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074739 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074739 #edb/ref 1061 39871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074739 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6cd88bb7-dfba-55b2-bb7a-0cce2188c27c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074739 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074740 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074740 #edb/ref 1061 39884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074740 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0ff59236-e8f5-575d-bc8b-731fac2b148a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074740 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074741 #edb/ref 1061 39885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074741 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "111068bf-412d-586d-b167-f6557ad36f93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074741 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075016 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 13 \\times 2\n$$\n\nFirst, we represent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nusing a model:\n\n![](images/f0/f08054646558ab944940f1f8f0d8a1077748f9882039e775c6cbdd4d307db102.png)\n\nMultiplication by a whole number is simply a repeated addition. And, since we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{3}\n$$\n\nby ${\\color{blue}2}$, we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}2$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/31/31fcef6d631262e49fc9c9297fd006f935fd7ee8182b0074d77ae22b626efbbc.png)\n\nThere are\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\times {\\color{red}1} = 2\n$$\n\nshaded pieces in total. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075016 #edb/ref 1065 6606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075016 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0baa395e-db83-5229-83f7-f40fd99e8a4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075016 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075017 #edb/ref 1064 "There's another way we can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the multiplication problem below:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10} \\times 7\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model to represent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\n![](images/62/62b50e658ed9bc691a9559548d09f87fbd3b33e007e81b5e78a3235ac4bc3280.png)\n\nNow, rather than repeating our model $7$ times (which would take a long time), we can instead multiply the number of shaded pieces by $7$.\n\nOur model has $1$ shaded piece. So, the resulting model must have\n\n$$\n1 \\times 7 = 7\n$$\n\nshaded pieces:\n\n![](images/2a/2a7d54b1d7a6a6013ee64b601d9dc0ba31c8a25dae0f5f69abba4b0fed0e8ee2.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\\times 7 = \\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075017 #edb/ref 1065 6608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075017 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5b799a98-0619-525c-b2da-433704f26bd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075017 #edb/ref 1067 "A Faster Way of Computing a Product Using Models" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
